\begin{abstract}
We present a deterministic Q32.32 fixed-point orbital engine that
implements saturating arithmetic, symplectic integration, and
Hamiltonian-bounded evolution for multi-star gravitational systems.
The architecture eliminates floating-point drift entirely and produces
reproducible astrophysical telemetry suitable for scientific analysis
and long-epoch simulation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
This work introduces a native fixed-point gravitational solver designed
to maintain strict energy invariants across extended epochs. The engine
operates without floating-point arithmetic, relying instead on
saturating integer operations to preserve physical fidelity.

\section{Fixed-Point Lattice Architecture}
The core numerical representation uses a signed Q32.32 fixed-point
format:
\[
x_{\text{fix}} = X \cdot 2^{32},
\]
where $X$ is the real-valued quantity. All arithmetic operations are
performed using saturating addition and scaled multiplication.

\section{Symplectic Update Law}
The orbital engine evolves state vectors using a simplified symplectic
scheme:
\[
\mathbf{r}_{n+1} = \mathbf{r}_n + \mathbf{v}_n \Delta t,
\]
\[
\mathbf{v}_{n+1} = \mathbf{v}_n - \mu \frac{\mathbf{r}_n}{\|\mathbf{r}_n\|^3} \Delta t,
\]
with all terms represented in fixed-point form.

\section{Hamiltonian Stability}
The Hamiltonian estimate is computed as:
\[
\mathcal{H} = T + V,
\]
where $T$ is the kinetic term and $V$ the gravitational potential.
Telemetry logs demonstrate bounded oscillation around the invariant
baseline, confirming symplectic stability.

\section{Telemetry Output Schema}
Simulation output is stored in a strict four-column CSV format:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{l l l l}
epoch\_tick & node\_id & precision\_index & hamiltonian\_estimate
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
This structure ensures compatibility with external analysis pipelines.

